inition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 241,\n      \"end_line\": 312,\n      \"event_count\": 48,\n      \"call_count\": 4,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 5\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"_process_plot_var_args._getdefaults\",\n      \"path\": \"matplotlib/axes/_base.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 320,\n      \"end_line\": 336,\n      \"event_count\": 13,\n      \"call_count\": 2,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 10\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_more\": true,\n  \"next_start\": 8\n}"}, {"content": "The `CalibrationDisplay.plot` method in `sklearn/calibration.py` appears to be closely related to plotting functionalities, especially in the context of calibration curves. This is a strong candidate for where the new feature might fit in.\n\nLet's search for more plotting-related artifacts in the `calibration.py` file specifically to get a broader view.\n\n<function=get_trace_artifacts>\n<parameter=path_contains>calibration.py</parameter>\n<parameter=name_contains>plot</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_trace_artifacts", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n{\n  \"instance_id\": \"scikit-learn__scikit-learn-17443\",\n  \"trace_summary\": {\n    \"unique_function_count\": 708,\n    \"returned_count\": 1,\n    \"filtered_count\": 1\n  },\n  \"applied_filters\": {\n    \"artifact_type\": \"all\",\n    \"path_contains\": \"calibration.py\",\n    \"name_contains\": \"plot\",\n    \"start\": 0,\n    \"limit\": 8\n  },\n  \"artifacts\": [\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"CalibrationDisplay.plot\",\n      \"path\": \"sklearn/calibration.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 1024,\n      \"end_line\": 1078,\n      \"event_count\": 23,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_more\": false,\n  \"next_start\": 1\n}"}, {"content": "Given the results, it seems that `CalibrationDisplay.plot` is the primary method related to plotting in the `calibration.py` file. This suggests that adding a calibration curve plot function could be integrated into or near this method.\n\nNext, let's examine the contents of `sklearn/calibration.py` to understand its structure and see where the new function might best fit.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/calibration.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/calibration.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/calibration.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/calibration.py`.\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"Calibration of predicted probabilities.\"\"\"\n  2 | \n  3 | # Author: Alexandre Gramfort <alexandre.gramfort@telecom-paristech.fr>\n  4 | #         Balazs Kegl <balazs.kegl@gmail.com>\n  5 | #         Jan Hendrik Metzen <jhm@informatik.uni-bremen.de>\n  6 | #         Mathieu Blondel <mathieu@mblondel.org>\n  7 | #\n  8 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n  9 | \n 10 | import warnings\n 11 | from inspect import signature\n 12 | from functools import partial\n 13 | \n 14 | from math import log\n 15 | import numpy as np\n 16 | from joblib import Parallel\n 17 | \n 18 | from scipy.special import expit\n 19 | from scipy.special import xlogy\n 20 | from scipy.optimize import fmin_bfgs\n 21 | \n 22 | from .base import (\n 23 |     BaseEstimator,\n 24 |     ClassifierMixin,\n 25 |     RegressorMixin,\n 26 |     clone,\n 27 |     MetaEstimatorMixin,\n 28 | )\n 29 | from .preprocessing import label_binarize, LabelEncoder\n 30 | from .utils import (\n 31 |     column_or_1d,\n 32 |     deprecated,\n 33 |     indexable,\n 34 | )\n 35 | \n 36 | from .utils.multiclass import check_classification_targets\n 37 | from .utils.fixes import delayed\n 38 | from .utils.validation import check_is_fitted, check_consistent_length\n 39 | from .utils.validation import _check_sample_weight, _num_samples\n 40 | from .utils import _safe_indexing\n 41 | from .isotonic import IsotonicRegression\n 42 | from .svm import LinearSVC\n 43 | from .model_selection import check_cv, cross_val_predict\n 44 | \n 45 | \n 46 | class CalibratedClassifierCV(ClassifierMixin, MetaEstimatorMixin, BaseEstimator):\n 47 |     \"\"\"Probability calibration with isotonic regression or logistic regression.\n 48 | \n 49 |     This class uses cross-validation to both estimate the parameters of a\n 50 |     classifier and subsequently calibrate a classifier. With default\n 51 |     `ensemble=True`, for each cv split it\n 52 |     fits a copy of the base estimator to the training subset, and calibrates it\n 53 |     using the testing subset. For prediction, predicted probabilities are\n 54 |     averaged across these individual calibrated classifiers. When\n 55 |     `ensemble=False`, cross-validation is used to obtain unbiased predictions,\n 56 |     via :func:`~sklearn.model_selection.cross_val_predict`, which are then\n 57 |     used for calibration. For prediction, the base estimator, trained using all\n 58 |     the data, is used. This is the method implemented when `probabilities=True`\n 59 |     for :mod:`sklearn.svm` estimators.\n 60 | \n 61 |     Already fitted classifiers can be calibrated via the parameter\n 62 |     `cv=\"prefit\"`. In this case, no cross-validation is used and all provided\n 63 |     data is used for calibration. The user has to take care manually that data\n 64 |     for model fitting and calibration are disjoint.\n 65 | \n 66 |     The calibration is based on the :term:`decision_function` method of the\n 67 |     `base_estimator` if it exists, else on :term:`predict_proba`.\n 68 | \n 69 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n 70 | \n 71 |     Parameters\n 72 |     ----------\n 73 |     base_estimator : estimator instance, default=None\n 74 |         The classifier whose output need to be calibrated to provide more\n 75 |         accurate `predict_proba` outputs. The default classifier is\n 76 |         a :class:`~sklearn.svm.LinearSVC`.\n 77 | \n 78 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}, default='sigmoid'\n 79 |         The method to use for calibration. Can be 'sigmoid' which\n 80 |         corresponds to Platt's method (i.e. a logistic regression model) or\n 81 |         'isotonic' which is a non-parametric approach. It is not advised to\n 82 |         use isotonic calibration with too few calibration samples\n 83 |         ``(<<1000)`` since it tends to overfit.\n 84 | \n 85 |     cv : int, cross-validation generator, iterable or \"prefit\", \\\n 86 |             default=None\n 87 |         Determines the cross-validation splitting strategy.\n 88 |         Possible inputs for cv are:\n 89 | \n 90 |         - None, to use the default 5-fold cross-validation,\n 91 |         - integer, to specify the number of folds.\n 92 |         - :term:`CV splitter`,\n 93 |         - An iterable yielding (train, test) splits as arrays of indices.\n 94 | \n 95 |         For integer/None inputs, if ``y`` is binary or multiclass,\n 96 |         :class:`~sklearn.model_selection.StratifiedKFold` is used. If ``y`` is\n 97 |         neither binary nor multiclass, :class:`~sklearn.model_selection.KFold`\n 98 |         is used.\n 99 | \n100 |         Refer to the :ref:`User Guide <cross_validation>` for the various\n101 |         cross-validation strategies that can be used here.\n102 | \n103 |         If \"prefit\" is passed, it is assumed that `base_estimator` has been\n104 |         fitted already and all data is used for calibration.\n105 | \n106 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.22\n107 |             ``cv`` default value if None changed from 3-fold to 5-fold.\n108 | \n109 |     n_jobs : int, default=None\n110 |         Number of jobs to run in parallel.\n111 |         ``None`` means 1 unless in a :obj:`joblib.parallel_backend` context.\n112 |         ``-1`` means using all processors.\n113 | \n114 |         Base estimator clones are fitted in parallel across cross-validation\n115 |         iterations. Therefore parallelism happens only when `cv != \"prefit\"`.\n116 | \n117 |         See :term:`Glossary <n_jobs>` for more details.\n118 | \n119 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n120 | \n121 |     ensemble : bool, default=True\n122 |         Determines how the calibrator is fitted when `cv` is not `'prefit'`.\n123 |         Ignored if `cv='prefit'`.\n124 | \n125 |         If `True`, the `base_estimator` is fitted using training data and\n126 |         calibrated using testing data, for each `cv` fold. The final estimator\n127 |         is an ensemble of `n_cv` fitted classifer and calibrator pairs, where\n128 |         `n_cv` is the number of cross-validation folds. The output is the\n129 |         average predicted probabilities of all pairs.\n130 | \n131 |         If `False`, `cv` is used to compute unbiased predictions, via\n132 |         :func:`~sklearn.model_selection.cross_val_predict`, which are then\n133 |         used for calibration. At prediction time, the classifier used is the\n134 |         `base_estimator` trained on all the data.\n135 |         Note that this method is also internally implemented  in\n136 |         :mod:`sklearn.svm` estimators with the `probabilities=True` parameter.\n137 | \n138 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n139 | \n140 |     Attributes\n141 |     ----------\n142 |     classes_ : ndarray of shape (n_classes,)\n143 |         The class labels.\n144 | \n145 |     n_features_in_ : int\n146 |         Number of features seen during :term:`fit`. Only defined if the\n147 |         underlying base_estimator exposes such an attribute when fit.\n148 | \n149 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n150 | \n151 |     feature_names_in_ : ndarray of shape (`n_features_in_`,)\n152 |         Names of features seen during :term:`fit`. Only defined if the\n153 |         underlying base_estimator exposes such an attribute when fit.\n154 | \n155 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n156 | \n157 |     calibrated_classifiers_ : list (len() equal to cv or 1 if `cv=\"prefit\"` \\\n158 |             or `ensemble=False`)\n159 |         The list of classifier and calibrator pairs.\n160 | \n161 |         - When `cv=\"prefit\"`, the fitted `base_estimator` and fitted\n162 |           calibrator.\n163 |         - When `cv` is not \"prefit\" and `ensemble=True`, `n_cv` fitted\n164 |           `base_estimator` and calibrator pairs. `n_cv` is the number of\n165 |           cross-validation folds.\n166 |         - When `cv` is not \"prefit\" and `ensemble=False`, the `base_estimator`,\n167 |           fitted on all the data, and fitted calibrator.\n168 | \n169 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.24\n170 |             Single calibrated classifier case when `ensemble=False`.\n171 | \n172 |     Examples\n173 |     --------\n174 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n175 |     >>> from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\n176 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV\n177 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(n_samples=100, n_features=2,\n178 |     ...                            n_redundant=0, random_state=42)\n179 |     >>> base_clf = GaussianNB()\n180 |     >>> calibrated_clf = CalibratedClassifierCV(base_estimator=base_clf, cv=3)\n181 |     >>> calibrated_clf.fit(X, y)\n182 |     CalibratedClassifierCV(base_estimator=GaussianNB(), cv=3)\n183 |     >>> len(calibrated_clf.calibrated_classifiers_)\n184 |     3\n185 |     >>> calibrated_clf.predict_proba(X)[:5, :]\n186 |     array([[0.110..., 0.889...],\n187 |            [0.072..., 0.927...],\n188 |            [0.928..., 0.071...],\n189 |            [0.928..., 0.071...],\n190 |            [0.071..., 0.928...]])\n191 | \n192 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n193 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(n_samples=100, n_features=2,\n194 |     ...                            n_redundant=0, random_state=42)\n195 |     >>> X_train, X_calib, y_train, y_calib = train_test_split(\n196 |     ...        X, y, random_state=42\n197 |     ... )\n198 |     >>> base_clf = GaussianNB()\n199 |     >>> base_clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n200 |     GaussianNB()\n201 |     >>> calibrated_clf = CalibratedClassifierCV(\n202 |     ...     base_estimator=base_clf,\n203 |     ...     cv=\"prefit\"\n204 |     ... )\n205 |     >>> calibrated_clf.fit(X_calib, y_calib)\n206 |     CalibratedClassifierCV(base_estimator=GaussianNB(), cv='prefit')\n207 |     >>> len(calibrated_clf.calibrated_classifiers_)\n208 |     1\n209 |     >>> calibrated_clf.predict_proba([[-0.5, 0.5]])\n210 |     array([[0.936..., 0.063...]])\n211 | \n212 |     References\n213 |     ----------\n214 |     .. [1] Obtaining calibrated probability estimates from decision trees\n215 |            and naive Bayesian classifiers, B. Zadrozny & C. Elkan, ICML 2001\n216 | \n217 |     .. [2] Transforming Classifier Scores into Accurate Multiclass\n218 |            Probability Estimates, B. Zadrozny & C. Elkan, (KDD 2002)\n219 | \n220 |     .. [3] Probabilistic Outputs for Support Vector Machines and Comparisons to\n221 |            Regularized Likelihood Methods, J. Platt, (1999)\n222 | \n223 |     .. [4] Predicting Good Probabilities with Supervised Learning,\n224 |            A. Niculescu-Mizil & R. Caruana, ICML 2005\n225 |     \"\"\"\n226 | \n227 |     def __init__(\n228 |         self,\n229 |         base_estimator=None,\n230 |         *,\n231 |         method=\"sigmoid\",\n232 |         cv=None,\n233 |         n_jobs=None,\n234 |         ensemble=True,\n235 |     ):\n236 |         self.base_estimator = base_estimator\n237 |         self.method = method\n238 |         self.cv = cv\n239 |         self.n_jobs = n_jobs\n240 |         self.ensemble = ensemble\n241 | \n242 |     def fit(self, X, y, sample_weight=None):\n243 |         \"\"\"Fit the calibrated model.\n244 | \n245 |         Parameters\n246 |         ----------\n247 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n248 |             Training data.\n249 | \n250 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n251 |             Target values.\n252 | \n253 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n254 |             Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n255 | \n256 |         Returns\n257 |         -------\n258 |         self : object\n259 |             Returns an instance of self.\n260 |         \"\"\"\n261 |         check_classification_targets(y)\n262 |         X, y = indexable(X, y)\n263 | \n264 |         if self.base_estimator is None:\n265 |             # we want all classifiers that don't expose a random_state\n266 |             # to be deterministic (and we don't want to expose this one).\n267 |             base_estimator = LinearSVC(random_state=0)\n268 |         else:\n269 |             base_estimator = self.base_estimator\n270 | \n271 |         self.calibrated_classifiers_ = []\n272 |         if self.cv == \"prefit\":\n273 |             # `classes_` should be consistent with that of base_estimator\n274 |             check_is_fitted(self.base_estimator, attributes=[\"classes_\"])\n275 |             self.classes_ = self.base_estimator.classes_\n276 | \n277 |             pred_method, method_name = _get_prediction_method(base_estimator)\n278 |             n_classes = len(self.classes_)\n279 |             predictions = _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes)\n280 | \n281 |             calibrated_classifier = _fit_calibrator(\n282 |                 base_estimator,\n283 |                 predictions,\n284 |                 y,\n285 |                 self.classes_,\n286 |                 self.method,\n287 |                 sample_weight,\n288 |             )\n289 |             self.calibrated_classifiers_.append(calibrated_classifier)\n290 |         else:\n291 |             # Set `classes_` using all `y`\n292 |             label_encoder_ = LabelEncoder().fit(y)\n293 |             self.classes_ = label_encoder_.classes_\n294 |             n_classes = len(self.classes_)\n295 | \n296 |             # sample_weight checks\n297 |             fit_parameters = signature(base_estimator.fit).parameters\n298 |             supports_sw = \"sample_weight\" in fit_parameters\n299 |             if sample_weight is not None:\n300 |                 sample_weight = _check_sample_weight(sample_weight, X)\n301 |                 if not supports_sw:\n302 |                     estimator_name = type(base_estimator).__name__\n303 |                     warnings.warn(\n304 |                         f\"Since {estimator_name} does not support \"\n305 |                         \"sample_weights, sample weights will only be\"\n306 |                         \" used for the calibration itself.\"\n307 |                     )\n308 | \n309 |             # Check that each cross-validation fold can have at least one\n310 |             # example per class\n311 |             if isinstance(self.cv, int):\n312 |                 n_folds = self.cv\n313 |             elif hasattr(self.cv, \"n_splits\"):\n314 |                 n_folds = self.cv.n_splits\n315 |             else:\n316 |                 n_folds = None\n317 |             if n_folds and np.any(\n318 |                 [np.sum(y == class_) < n_folds for class_ in self.classes_]\n319 |             ):\n320 |                 raise ValueError(\n321 |                     f\"Requesting {n_folds}-fold \"\n322 |                     \"cross-validation but provided less than \"\n323 |                     f\"{n_folds} examples for at least one class.\"\n324 |                 )\n325 |             cv = check_cv(self.cv, y, classifier=True)\n326 | \n327 |             if self.ensemble:\n328 |                 parallel = Parallel(n_jobs=self.n_jobs)\n329 | \n330 |                 self.calibrated_classifiers_ = parallel(\n331 |                     delayed(_fit_classifier_calibrator_pair)(\n332 |                         clone(base_estimator),\n333 |                         X,\n334 |                         y,\n335 |                         train=train,\n336 |                         test=test,\n337 |                         method=self.method,\n338 |                         classes=self.classes_,\n339 |                         supports_sw=supports_sw,\n340 |                         sample_weight=sample_weight,\n341 |                     )\n342 |                     for train, test in cv.split(X, y)\n343 |                 )\n344 |             else:\n345 |                 this_estimator = clone(base_estimator)\n346 |                 _, method_name = _get_prediction_method(this_estimator)\n347 |                 pred_method = partial(\n348 |                     cross_val_predict,\n349 |                     estimator=this_estimator,\n350 |                     X=X,\n351 |                     y=y,\n352 |                     cv=cv,\n353 |                     method=method_name,\n354 |                     n_jobs=self.n_jobs,\n355 |                 )\n356 |                 predictions = _compute_predictions(\n357 |                     pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes\n358 |                 )\n359 | \n360 |                 if sample_weight is not None and supports_sw:\n361 |                     this_estimator.fit(X, y, sample_weight)\n362 |                 else:\n363 |                     this_estimator.fit(X, y)\n364 |                 calibrated_classifier = _fit_calibrator(\n365 |                     this_estimator,\n366 |                     predictions,\n367 |                     y,\n368 |                     self.classes_,\n369 |                     self.method,\n370 |                     sample_weight,\n371 |                 )\n372 |                 self.calibrated_classifiers_.append(calibrated_classifier)\n373 | \n374 |         first_clf = self.calibrated_classifiers_[0].base_estimator\n375 |         if hasattr(first_clf, \"n_features_in_\"):\n376 |             self.n_features_in_ = first_clf.n_features_in_\n377 |         if hasattr(first_clf, \"feature_names_in_\"):\n378 |             self.feature_names_in_ = first_clf.feature_names_in_\n379 |         return self\n380 | \n381 |     def predict_proba(self, X):\n382 |         \"\"\"Calibrated probabilities of classification.\n383 | \n384 |         This function returns calibrated probabilities of classification\n385 |         according to each class on an array of test vectors X.\n386 | \n387 |         Parameters\n388 |         ----------\n389 |         X : The samples, as accepted by base_estimator.predict_proba\n390 | \n391 |         Returns\n392 |         -------\n393 |         C : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_classes)\n394 |             The predicted probas.\n395 |         \"\"\"\n396 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n397 |         # Compute the arithmetic mean of the predictions of the calibrated\n398 |         # classifiers\n399 |         mean_proba = np.zeros((_num_samples(X), len(self.classes_)))\n400 |         for calibrated_classifier in self.calibrated_classifiers_:\n401 |             proba = calibrated_classifier.predict_proba(X)\n402 |             mean_proba += proba\n403 | \n404 |         mean_proba /= len(self.calibrated_classifiers_)\n405 | \n406 |         return mean_proba\n407 | \n408 |     def predict(self, X):\n409 |         \"\"\"Predict the target of new samples. The predicted class is the\n410 |         class that has the highest probability, and can thus be different\n411 |         from the prediction of the uncalibrated classifier.\n412 | \n413 |         Parameters\n414 |         ----------\n415 |         X : The samples, as accepted by base_estimator.predict\n416 | \n417 |         Returns\n418 |         -------\n419 |         C : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n420 |             The predicted class.\n421 |         \"\"\"\n422 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n423 |         return self.classes_[np.argmax(self.predict_proba(X), axis=1)]\n424 | \n425 |     def _more_tags(self):\n426 |         return {\n427 |             \"_xfail_checks\": {\n428 |                 \"check_sample_weights_invariance\": (\n429 |                     \"zero sample_weight is not equivalent to removing samples\"\n430 |                 ),\n431 |             }\n432 |         }\n433 | \n434 | \n435 | def _fit_classifier_calibrator_pair(\n436 |     estimator, X, y, train, test, supports_sw, method, classes, sample_weight=None\n437 | ):\n438 |     \"\"\"Fit a classifier/calibration pair on a given train/test split.\n439 | \n440 |     Fit the classifier on the train set, compute its predictions on the test\n441 |     set and use the predictions as input to fit the calibrator along with the\n442 |     test labels.\n443 | \n444 |     Parameters\n445 |     ----------\n446 |     estimator : estimator instance\n447 |         Cloned base estimator.\n448 | \n449 |     X : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_features)\n450 |         Sample data.\n451 | \n452 |     y : array-like, shape (n_samples,)\n453 |         Targets.\n454 | \n455 |     train : ndarray, shape (n_train_indicies,)\n456 |         Indices of the training subset.\n457 | \n458 |     test : ndarray, shape (n_test_indicies,)\n459 |         Indices of the testing subset.\n460 | \n461 |     supports_sw : bool\n462 |         Whether or not the `estimator` supports sample weights.\n463 | \n464 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}\n465 |         Method to use for calibration.\n466 | \n467 |     classes : ndarray, shape (n_classes,)\n468 |         The target classes.\n469 | \n470 |     sample_weight : array-like, default=None\n471 |         Sample weights for `X`.\n472 | \n473 |     Returns\n474 |     -------\n475 |     calibrated_classifier : _CalibratedClassifier instance\n476 |     \"\"\"\n477 |     X_train, y_train = _safe_indexing(X, train), _safe_indexing(y, train)\n478 |     X_test, y_test = _safe_indexing(X, test), _safe_indexing(y, test)\n479 |     if supports_sw and sample_weight is not None:\n480 |         sw_train = _safe_indexing(sample_weight, train)\n481 |         sw_test = _safe_indexing(sample_weight, test)\n482 |     else:\n483 |         sw_train = None\n484 |         sw_test = None\n485 | \n486 |     if supports_sw:\n487 |         estimator.fit(X_train, y_train, sample_weight=sw_train)\n488 |     else:\n489 |         estimator.fit(X_train, y_train)\n490 | \n491 |     n_classes = len(classes)\n492 |     pred_method, method_name = _get_prediction_method(estimator)\n493 |     predictions = _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X_test, n_classes)\n494 | \n495 |     calibrated_classifier = _fit_calibrator(\n496 |         estimator, predictions, y_test, classes, method, sample_weight=sw_test\n497 |     )\n498 |     return calibrated_classifier\n499 | \n500 | \n501 | def _get_prediction_method(clf):\n502 |     \"\"\"Return prediction method.\n503 | \n504 |     `decision_function` method of `clf` returned, if it\n505 |     exists, otherwise `predict_proba` method returned.\n506 | \n507 |     Parameters\n508 |     ----------\n509 |     clf : Estimator instance\n510 |         Fitted classifier to obtain the prediction method from.\n511 | \n512 |     Returns\n513 |     -------\n514 |     prediction_method : callable\n515 |         The prediction method.\n516 |     method_name : str\n517 |         The name of the prediction method.\n518 |     \"\"\"\n519 |     if hasattr(clf, \"decision_function\"):\n520 |         method = getattr(clf, \"decision_function\")\n521 |         return method, \"decision_function\"\n522 |     elif hasattr(clf, \"predict_proba\"):\n523 |         method = getattr(clf, \"predict_proba\")\n524 |         return method, \"predict_proba\"\n525 |     else:\n526 |         raise RuntimeError(\n527 |             \"'base_estimator' has no 'decision_function' or 'predict_proba' method.\"\n528 |         )\n529 | \n530 | \n531 | def _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes):\n532 |     \"\"\"Return predictions for `X` and reshape binary outputs to shape\n533 |     (n_samples, 1).\n534 | \n535 |     Parameters\n536 |     ----------\n537 |     pred_method : callable\n538 |         Prediction method.\n539 | \n540 |     method_name: str\n541 |         Name of the prediction method\n542 | \n543 |     X : array-like or None\n544 |         Data used to obtain predictions.\n545 | \n546 |     n_classes : int\n547 |         Number of classes present.\n548 | \n549 |     Returns\n550 |     -------\n551 |     predictions : array-like, shape (X.shape[0], len(clf.classes_))\n552 |         The predictions. Note if there are 2 classes, array is of shape\n553 |         (X.shape[0], 1).\n554 |     \"\"\"\n555 |     predictions = pred_method(X=X)\n556 | \n557 |     if method_name == \"decision_function\":\n558 |         if predictions.ndim == 1:\n559 |             predictions = predictions[:, np.newaxis]\n560 |     elif method_name == \"predict_proba\":\n561 |         if n_classes == 2:\n562 |             predictions = predictions[:, 1:]\n563 |     else:  # pragma: no cover\n564 |         # this branch should be unreachable.\n565 |         raise ValueError(f\"Invalid prediction method: {method_name}\")\n566 |     return predictions\n567 | \n568 | \n569 | def _fit_calibrator(clf, predictions, y, classes, method, sample_weight=None):\n570 |     \"\"\"Fit calibrator(s) and return a `_CalibratedClassifier`\n571 |     instance.\n572 | \n573 |     `n_classes` (i.e. `len(clf.classes_)`) calibrators are fitted.\n574 |     However, if `n_classes` equals 2, one calibrator is fitted.\n575 | \n576 |     Parameters\n577 |     ----------\n578 |     clf : estimator instance\n579 |         Fitted classifier.\n580 | \n581 |     predictions : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_classes) or (n_samples, 1) \\\n582 |                     when binary.\n583 |         Raw predictions returned by the un-calibrated base classifier.\n584 | \n585 |     y : array-like, shape (n_samples,)\n586 |         The targets.\n587 | \n588 |     classes : ndarray, shape (n_classes,)\n589 |         All the prediction classes.\n590 | \n591 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}\n592 |         The method to use for calibration.\n593 | \n594 |     sample_weight : ndarray, shape (n_samples,), default=None\n595 |         Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n596 | \n597 |     Returns\n598 |     -------\n599 |     pipeline : _CalibratedClassifier instance\n600 |     \"\"\"\n601 |     Y = label_binarize(y, classes=classes)\n602 |     label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(classes)\n603 |     pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(clf.classes_)\n604 |     calibrators = []\n605 |     for class_idx, this_pred in zip(pos_class_indices, predictions.T):\n606 |         if method == \"isotonic\":\n607 |             calibrator = IsotonicRegression(out_of_bounds=\"clip\")\n608 |         elif method == \"sigmoid\":\n609 |             calibrator = _SigmoidCalibration()\n610 |         else:\n611 |             raise ValueError(\n612 |                 f\"'method' should be one of: 'sigmoid' or 'isotonic'. Got {method}.\"\n613 |             )\n614 |         calibrator.fit(this_pred, Y[:, class_idx], sample_weight)\n615 |         calibrators.append(calibrator)\n616 | \n617 |     pipeline = _CalibratedClassifier(clf, calibrators, method=method, classes=classes)\n618 |     return pipeline\n619 | \n620 | \n621 | class _CalibratedClassifier:\n622 |     \"\"\"Pipeline-like chaining a fitted classifier and its fitted calibrators.\n623 | \n624 |     Parameters\n625 |     ----------\n626 |     base_estimator : estimator instance\n627 |         Fitted classifier.\n628 | \n629 |     calibrators : list of fitted estimator instances\n630 |         List of fitted calibrators (either 'IsotonicRegression' or\n631 |         '_SigmoidCalibration'). The number of calibrators equals the number of\n632 |         classes. However, if there are 2 classes, the list contains only one\n633 |         fitted calibrator.\n634 | \n635 |     classes : array-like of shape (n_classes,)\n636 |         All the prediction classes.\n637 | \n638 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}, default='sigmoid'\n639 |         The method to use for calibration. Can be 'sigmoid' which\n640 |         corresponds to Platt's method or 'isotonic' which is a\n641 |         non-parametric approach based on isotonic regression.\n642 | \n643 |     Attributes\n644 |     ----------\n645 |     calibrators_ : list of fitted estimator instances\n646 |         Same as `calibrators`. Exposed for backward-compatibility. Use\n647 |         `calibrators` instead.\n648 | \n649 |         .. deprecated:: 0.24\n650 |            `calibrators_` is deprecated from 0.24 and will be removed in\n651 |            1.1 (renaming of 0.26). Use `calibrators` instead.\n652 |     \"\"\"\n653 | \n654 |     def __init__(self, base_estimator, calibrators, *, classes, method=\"sigmoid\"):\n655 |         self.base_estimator = base_estimator\n656 |         self.calibrators = calibrators\n657 |         self.classes = classes\n658 |         self.method = method\n659 | \n660 |     # TODO: Remove in 1.1\n661 |     # mypy error: Decorated property not supported\n662 |     @deprecated(  # type: ignore\n663 |         \"`calibrators_` is deprecated in 0.24 and will be removed in 1.1\"\n664 |         \"(renaming of 0.26). Use `calibrators` instead.\"\n665 |     )\n666 |     @property\n667 |     def calibrators_(self):\n668 |         return self.calibrators\n669 | \n670 |     def predict_proba(self, X):\n671 |         \"\"\"Calculate calibrated probabilities.\n672 | \n673 |         Calculates classification calibrated probabilities\n674 |         for each class, in a one-vs-all manner, for `X`.\n675 | \n676 |         Parameters\n677 |         ----------\n678 |         X : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n679 |             The sample data.\n680 | \n681 |         Returns\n682 |         -------\n683 |         proba : array, shape (n_samples, n_classes)\n684 |             The predicted probabilities. Can be exact zeros.\n685 |         \"\"\"\n686 |         n_classes = len(self.classes)\n687 |         pred_method, method_name = _get_prediction_method(self.base_estimator)\n688 |         predictions = _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes)\n689 | \n690 |         label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(self.classes)\n691 |         pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(self.base_estimator.classes_)\n692 | \n693 |         proba = np.zeros((_num_samples(X), n_classes))\n694 |         for class_idx, this_pred, calibrator in zip(\n695 |             pos_class_indices, predictions.T, self.calibrators\n696 |         ):\n697 |             if n_classes == 2:\n698 |                 # When binary, `predictions` consists only of predictions for\n699 |                 # clf.classes_[1] but `pos_class_indices` = 0\n700 |                 class_idx += 1\n701 |             proba[:, class_idx] = calibrator.predict(this_pred)\n702 | \n703 |         # Normalize the probabilities\n704 |         if n_classes == 2:\n705 |             proba[:, 0] = 1.0 - proba[:, 1]\n706 |         else:\n707 |             denominator = np.sum(proba, axis=1)[:, np.newaxis]\n708 |             # In the edge case where for each class calibrator returns a null\n709 |             # probability for a given sample, use the uniform distribution\n710 |             # instead.\n711 |             uniform_proba = np.full_like(proba, 1 / n_classes)\n712 |             proba = np.divide(\n713 |                 proba, denominator, out=uniform_proba, where=denominator != 0\n714 |             )\n715 | \n716 |         # Deal with cases where the predicted probability minimally exceeds 1.0\n717 |         proba[(1.0 < proba) & (proba <= 1.0 + 1e-5)] = 1.0\n718 | \n719 |         return proba\n720 | \n721 | \n722 | def _sigmoid_calibration(predictions, y, sample_weight=None):\n723 |     \"\"\"Probability Calibration with sigmoid method (Platt 2000)\n724 | \n725 |     Parameters\n726 |     ----------\n727 |     predictions : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n728 |         The decision function or predict proba for the samples.\n729 | \n730 |     y : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n731 |         The targets.\n732 | \n733 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n734 |         Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n735 | \n736 |     Returns\n737 |     -------\n738 |     a : float\n739 |         The slope.\n740 | \n741 |     b : float\n742 |         The intercept.\n743 | \n744 |     References\n745 |     ----------\n746 |     Platt, \"Probabilistic Outputs for Support Vector Machines\"\n747 |     \"\"\"\n748 |     predictions = column_or_1d(predictions)\n749 |     y = column_or_1d(y)\n750 | \n751 |     F = predictions  # F follows Platt's notations\n752 | \n753 |     # Bayesian priors (see Platt end of section 2.2)\n754 |     prior0 = float(np.sum(y <= 0))\n755 |     prior1 = y.shape[0] - prior0\n756 |     T = np.zeros(y.shape)\n757 |     T[y > 0] = (prior1 + 1.0) / (prior1 + 2.0)\n758 |     T[y <= 0] = 1.0 / (prior0 + 2.0)\n759 |     T1 = 1.0 - T\n760 | \n761 |     def objective(AB):\n762 |         # From Platt (beginning of Section 2.2)\n763 |         P = expit(-(AB[0] * F + AB[1]))\n764 |         loss = -(xlogy(T, P) + xlogy(T1, 1.0 - P))\n765 |         if sample_weight is not None:\n766 |             return (sample_weight * loss).sum()\n767 |         else:\n768 |             return loss.sum()\n769 | \n770 |     def grad(AB):\n771 |         # gradient of the objective function\n772 |         P = expit(-(AB[0] * F + AB[1]))\n773 |         TEP_minus_T1P = T - P\n774 |         if sample_weight is not None:\n775 |             TEP_minus_T1P *= sample_weight\n776 |         dA = np.dot(TEP_minus_T1P, F)\n777 |         dB = np.sum(TEP_minus_T1P)\n778 |         return np.array([dA, dB])\n779 | \n780 |     AB0 = np.array([0.0, log((prior0 + 1.0) / (prior1 + 1.0))])\n781 |     AB_ = fmin_bfgs(objective, AB0, fprime=grad, disp=False)\n782 |     return AB_[0], AB_[1]\n783 | \n784 | \n785 | class _SigmoidCalibration(RegressorMixin, BaseEstimator):\n786 |     \"\"\"Sigmoid regression model.\n787 | \n788 |     Attributes\n789 |     ----------\n790 |     a_ : float\n791 |         The slope.\n792 | \n793 |     b_ : float\n794 |         The intercept.\n795 |     \"\"\"\n796 | \n797 |     def fit(self, X, y, sample_weight=None):\n798 |         \"\"\"Fit the model using X, y as training data.\n799 | \n800 |         Parameters\n801 |         ----------\n802 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n803 |             Training data.\n804 | \n805 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n806 |             Training target.\n807 | \n808 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n809 |             Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n810 | \n811 |         Returns\n812 |         -------\n813 |         self : object\n814 |             Returns an instance of self.\n815 |         \"\"\"\n816 |         X = column_or_1d(X)\n817 |         y = column_or_1d(y)\n818 |         X, y = indexable(X, y)\n819 | \n820 |         self.a_, self.b_ = _sigmoid_calibration(X, y, sample_weight)\n821 |         return self\n822 | \n823 |     def predict(self, T):\n824 |         \"\"\"Predict new data by linear interpolation.\n825 | \n826 |         Parameters\n827 |         ----------\n828 |         T : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n829 |             Data to predict from.\n830 | \n831 |         Returns\n832 |         -------\n833 |         T_ : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n834 |             The predicted data.\n835 |         \"\"\"\n836 |         T = column_or_1d(T)\n837 |         return expit(-(self.a_ * T + self.b_))\n838 | \n839 | \n840 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n946 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the provided code snippet, it appears that the `calibration_curve` function in `sklearn/calibration.py` is relevant for generating the data needed for a calibration curve plot. Additionally, the `CalibrationDisplay` class and its `plot` method are good candidates for where a new calibration curve plotting function could fit in.\n\nLet's also look into the `sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py` and `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py` files, as they might contain the infrastructure for plotting and could be a suitable place for adding a new plotting function.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py', 'sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py`.\n\n\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py`.\n```\n  1 | import numpy as np\n  2 | \n  3 | from ...base import is_classifier\n  4 | \n  5 | \n  6 | def _check_classifier_response_method(estimator, response_method):\n  7 |     \"\"\"Return prediction method from the response_method\n  8 | \n  9 |     Parameters\n 10 |     ----------\n 11 |     estimator: object\n 12 |         Classifier to check\n 13 | \n 14 |     response_method: {'auto', 'predict_proba', 'decision_function'}\n 15 |         Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n 16 |         :term:`decision_function` as the target response. If set to 'auto',\n 17 |         :term:`predict_proba` is tried first and if it does not exist\n 18 |         :term:`decision_function` is tried next.\n 19 | \n 20 |     Returns\n 21 |     -------\n 22 |     prediction_method: callable\n 23 |         prediction method of estimator\n 24 |     \"\"\"\n 25 | \n 26 |     if response_method not in (\"predict_proba\", \"decision_function\", \"auto\"):\n 27 |         raise ValueError(\n 28 |             \"response_method must be 'predict_proba', 'decision_function' or 'auto'\"\n 29 |         )\n 30 | \n 31 |     error_msg = \"response method {} is not defined in {}\"\n 32 |     if response_method != \"auto\":\n 33 |         prediction_method = getattr(estimator, response_method, None)\n 34 |         if prediction_method is None:\n 35 |             raise ValueError(\n 36 |                 error_msg.format(response_method, estimator.__class__.__name__)\n 37 |             )\n 38 |     else:\n 39 |         predict_proba = getattr(estimator, \"predict_proba\", None)\n 40 |         decision_function = getattr(estimator, \"decision_function\", None)\n 41 |         prediction_method = predict_proba or decision_function\n 42 |         if prediction_method is None:\n 43 |             raise ValueError(\n 44 |                 error_msg.format(\n 45 |                     \"decision_function or predict_proba\", estimator.__class__.__name__\n 46 |                 )\n 47 |             )\n 48 | \n 49 |     return prediction_method\n 50 | \n 51 | \n 52 | def _get_response(X, estimator, response_method, pos_label=None):\n 53 |     \"\"\"Return response and positive label.\n 54 | \n 55 |     Parameters\n 56 |     ----------\n 57 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 58 |         Input values.\n 59 | \n 60 |     estimator : estimator instance\n 61 |         Fitted classifier or a fitted :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`\n 62 |         in which the last estimator is a classifier.\n 63 | \n 64 |     response_method: {'auto', 'predict_proba', 'decision_function'}\n 65 |         Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n 66 |         :term:`decision_function` as the target response. If set to 'auto',\n 67 |         :term:`predict_proba` is tried first and if it does not exist\n 68 |         :term:`decision_function` is tried next.\n 69 | \n 70 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 71 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing\n 72 |         the metrics. By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is\n 73 |         considered as the positive class.\n 74 | \n 75 |     Returns\n 76 |     -------\n 77 |     y_pred: ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n 78 |         Target scores calculated from the provided response_method\n 79 |         and pos_label.\n 80 | \n 81 |     pos_label: str or int\n 82 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing\n 83 |         the metrics.\n 84 |     \"\"\"\n 85 |     classification_error = (\n 86 |         \"Expected 'estimator' to be a binary classifier, but got\"\n 87 |         f\" {estimator.__class__.__name__}\"\n 88 |     )\n 89 | \n 90 |     if not is_classifier(estimator):\n 91 |         raise ValueError(classification_error)\n 92 | \n 93 |     prediction_method = _check_classifier_response_method(estimator, response_method)\n 94 | \n 95 |     y_pred = prediction_method(X)\n 96 | \n 97 |     if pos_label is not None and pos_label not in estimator.classes_:\n 98 |         raise ValueError(\n 99 |             \"The class provided by 'pos_label' is unknown. Got \"\n100 |             f\"{pos_label} instead of one of {estimator.classes_}\"\n101 |         )\n102 | \n103 |     if y_pred.ndim != 1:  # `predict_proba`\n104 |         if y_pred.shape[1] != 2:\n105 |             raise ValueError(classification_error)\n106 |         if pos_label is None:\n107 |             pos_label = estimator.classes_[1]\n108 |             y_pred = y_pred[:, 1]\n109 |         else:\n110 |             class_idx = np.flatnonzero(estimator.classes_ == pos_label)\n111 |             y_pred = y_pred[:, class_idx]\n112 |     else:\n113 |         if pos_label is None:\n114 |             pos_label = estimator.classes_[1]\n115 |         elif pos_label == estimator.classes_[0]:\n116 |             y_pred *= -1\n117 | \n118 |     return y_pred, pos_label\n119 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "```\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 440\nfunction: calibration_curve\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py\nclass: CalibrationDisplay\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py\nline: 64\nfunction: _get_response\n\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py\nline: 1085\nfunction: check_matplotlib_support\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py\nclass: ConfusionMatrixDisplay\n```\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_data_dependencies", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n- '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.  \n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\nArgs:\n- `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names. \n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes. \n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`. \n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope. \n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. \n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n- `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols. \n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n\nImportant behavior:\n- If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n- If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n- `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n- `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n- `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n- `symbol`: The symbol name.\n- `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n- `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n\nEach edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"symbol_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.", "default": null}, "scope_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.", "default": null}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.", "default": null}, "edge_type": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 12, "description": "Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice."}, "path_limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 4, "description": "Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 331155, "completion_tokens": 876}, "time": 264.09343099594116}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-24145", "found_files": [["sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py", "sklearn/utils/extmath.py", "sklearn/utils/validation.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer", "sklearn/utils/extmath.py:safe_sparse_dot", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_array"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.fit", "sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.transform", "sklearn/utils/extmath.py:safe_sparse_dot", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_array"]], "raw_output_loc": ["```\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: fit\n\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: transform\n\nsklearn/utils/extmath.py\nfunction: safe_sparse_dot\n\nsklearn/utils/sparsefuncs_fast.py\nfunction: csr_mean_variance_axis0\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nfunction: check_array\n```"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "55af30d981ea2f72346ff93602f0b3b740cfe8d6", "problem_statement": "Add sparse matrix output to SplineTransformer\n### Describe the workflow you want to enable\n\nAs B-splines naturally have a sparse structure, I'd like to have the option that `SplineTransformer` returns a sparse matrix instead of always an ndarray.\r\n```python\r\nimport numpy as np\r\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import SplineTransformer\r\n\r\nX = np.arange(6).reshape(6, 1)\r\nspline = SplineTransformer(degree=2, n_knots=3, sparse=True)\r\nspline.fit_transform(X)\r\n```\n\n### Describe your proposed solution\n\nWith scipy >= 1.8 (yet to be released), we can use `design_matrix` from https://github.com/scipy/scipy/pull/14344.\n\n### Describe alternatives you've considered, if relevant\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n", "patch": "diff --git a/doc/whats_new/v1.3.rst b/doc/whats_new/v1.3.rst\nindex 9cab0db995c5d..ec1301844b877 100644\n--- a/doc/whats_new/v1.3.rst\n+++ b/doc/whats_new/v1.3.rst\n@@ -487,6 +487,11 @@ Changelog\n   categorical encoding based on target mean conditioned on the value of the\n   category. :pr:`25334` by `Thomas Fan`_.\n \n+- |Enhancement| A new parameter `sparse_output` was added to\n+  :class:`SplineTransformer`, available as of SciPy 1.8. If `sparse_output=True`,\n+  :class:`SplineTransformer` returns a sparse CSR matrix.\n+  :pr:`24145` by :user:`Christian Lorentzen <lorentzenchr>`.\n+\n - |Enhancement| Adds a `feature_name_combiner` parameter to\n   :class:`preprocessing.OneHotEncoder`. This specifies a custom callable to create\n   feature names to be returned by :meth:`get_feature_names_out`.\ndiff --git a/sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py b/sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nindex 64ecb9864fae0..f379ee9135706 100644\n--- a/sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\n+++ b/sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\n@@ -13,11 +13,11 @@\n \n from ..base import BaseEstimator, TransformerMixin\n from ..utils import check_array\n+from ..utils.fixes import sp_version, parse_version\n from ..utils.validation import check_is_fitted, FLOAT_DTYPES, _check_sample_weight\n from ..utils.validation import _check_feature_names_in\n from ..utils._param_validation import Interval, StrOptions\n from ..utils.stats import _weighted_percentile\n-from ..utils.fixes import sp_version, parse_version\n \n from ._csr_polynomial_expansion import (\n     _csr_polynomial_expansion,\n@@ -574,8 +574,6 @@ def transform(self, X):\n         return XP\n \n \n-# TODO:\n-# - sparse support (either scipy or own cython solution)?\n class SplineTransformer(TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n     \"\"\"Generate univariate B-spline bases for features.\n \n@@ -635,8 +633,14 @@ class SplineTransformer(TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n         i.e. a column of ones. It acts as an intercept term in a linear models.\n \n     order : {'C', 'F'}, default='C'\n-        Order of output array. 'F' order is faster to compute, but may slow\n-        down subsequent estimators.\n+        Order of output array in the dense case. `'F'` order is faster to compute, but\n+        may slow down subsequent estimators.\n+\n+    sparse_output : bool, default=False\n+        Will return sparse CSR matrix if set True else will return an array. This\n+        option is only available with `scipy>=1.8`.\n+\n+        .. versionadded:: 1.2\n \n     Attributes\n     ----------\n@@ -699,6 +703,7 @@ class SplineTransformer(TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n         ],\n         \"include_bias\": [\"boolean\"],\n         \"order\": [StrOptions({\"C\", \"F\"})],\n+        \"sparse_output\": [\"boolean\"],\n     }\n \n     def __init__(\n@@ -710,6 +715,7 @@ def __init__(\n         extrapolation=\"constant\",\n         include_bias=True,\n         order=\"C\",\n+        sparse_output=False,\n     ):\n         self.n_knots = n_knots\n         self.degree = degree\n@@ -717,6 +723,7 @@ def __init__(\n         self.extrapolation = extrapolation\n         self.include_bias = include_bias\n         self.order = order\n+        self.sparse_output = sparse_output\n \n     @staticmethod\n     def _get_base_knot_positions(X, n_knots=10, knots=\"uniform\", sample_weight=None):\n@@ -843,6 +850,12 @@ def fit(self, X, y=None, sample_weight=None):\n             elif not np.all(np.diff(base_knots, axis=0) > 0):\n                 raise ValueError(\"knots must be sorted without duplicates.\")\n \n+        if self.sparse_output and sp_version < parse_version(\"1.8.0\"):\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                \"Option sparse_output=True is only available with scipy>=1.8.0, \"\n+                f\"but here scipy=={sp_version} is used.\"\n+            )\n+\n         # number of knots for base interval\n         n_knots = base_knots.shape[0]\n \n@@ -934,7 +947,7 @@ def transform(self, X):\n \n         Returns\n         -------\n-        XBS : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features * n_splines)\n+        XBS : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features * n_splines)\n             The matrix of features, where n_splines is the number of bases\n             elements of the B-splines, n_knots + degree - 1.\n         \"\"\"\n@@ -946,6 +959,19 @@ def transform(self, X):\n         n_splines = self.bsplines_[0].c.shape[1]\n         degree = self.degree\n \n+        # TODO: Remove this condition, once scipy 1.10 is the minimum version.\n+        #       Only scipy => 1.10 supports design_matrix(.., extrapolate=..).\n+        #       The default (implicit in scipy < 1.10) is extrapolate=False.\n+        scipy_1_10 = sp_version >= parse_version(\"1.10.0\")\n+        # Note: self.bsplines_[0].extrapolate is True for extrapolation in\n+        # [\"periodic\", \"continue\"]\n+        if scipy_1_10:\n+            use_sparse = self.sparse_output\n+            kwargs_extrapolate = {\"extrapolate\": self.bsplines_[0].extrapolate}\n+        else:\n+            use_sparse = self.sparse_output and not self.bsplines_[0].extrapolate\n+            kwargs_extrapolate = dict()\n+\n         # Note that scipy BSpline returns float64 arrays and converts input\n         # x=X[:, i] to c-contiguous float64.\n         n_out = self.n_features_out_ + n_features * (1 - self.include_bias)\n@@ -953,7 +979,10 @@ def transform(self, X):\n             dtype = X.dtype\n         else:\n             dtype = np.float64\n-        XBS = np.zeros((n_samples, n_out), dtype=dtype, order=self.order)\n+        if use_sparse:\n+            output_list = []\n+        else:\n+            XBS = np.zeros((n_samples, n_out), dtype=dtype, order=self.order)\n \n         for i in range(n_features):\n             spl = self.bsplines_[i]\n@@ -972,20 +1001,53 @@ def transform(self, X):\n                 else:\n                     x = X[:, i]\n \n-                XBS[:, (i * n_splines) : ((i + 1) * n_splines)] = spl(x)\n-\n-            else:\n-                xmin = spl.t[degree]\n-                xmax = spl.t[-degree - 1]\n+                if use_sparse:\n+                    XBS_sparse = BSpline.design_matrix(\n+                        x, spl.t, spl.k, **kwargs_extrapolate\n+                    )\n+                    if self.extrapolation == \"periodic\":\n+                        # See the construction of coef in fit. We need to add the last\n+                        # degree spline basis function to the first degree ones and\n+                        # then drop the last ones.\n+                        # Note: See comment about SparseEfficiencyWarning below.\n+                        XBS_sparse = XBS_sparse.tolil()\n+                        XBS_sparse[:, :degree] += XBS_sparse[:, -degree:]\n+                        XBS_sparse = XBS_sparse[:, :-degree]\n+                else:\n+                    XBS[:, (i * n_splines) : ((i + 1) * n_splines)] = spl(x)\n+            else:  # extrapolation in (\"constant\", \"linear\")\n+                xmin, xmax = spl.t[degree], spl.t[-degree - 1]\n+                # spline values at boundaries\n+                f_min, f_max = spl(xmin), spl(xmax)\n                 mask = (xmin <= X[:, i]) & (X[:, i] <= xmax)\n-                XBS[mask, (i * n_splines) : ((i + 1) * n_splines)] = spl(X[mask, i])\n+                if use_sparse:\n+                    mask_inv = ~mask\n+                    x = X[:, i].copy()\n+                    # Set some arbitrary values outside boundary that will be reassigned\n+                    # later.\n+                    x[mask_inv] = spl.t[self.degree]\n+                    XBS_sparse = BSpline.design_matrix(x, spl.t, spl.k)\n+                    # Note: Without converting to lil_matrix we would get:\n+                    # scipy.sparse._base.SparseEfficiencyWarning: Changing the sparsity\n+                    # structure of a csr_matrix is expensive. lil_matrix is more\n+                    # efficient.\n+                    if np.any(mask_inv):\n+                        XBS_sparse = XBS_sparse.tolil()\n+                        XBS_sparse[mask_inv, :] = 0\n+                else:\n+                    XBS[mask, (i * n_splines) : ((i + 1) * n_splines)] = spl(X[mask, i])\n \n             # Note for extrapolation:\n             # 'continue' is already returned as is by scipy BSplines\n             if self.extrapolation == \"error\":\n                 # BSpline with extrapolate=False does not raise an error, but\n-                # output np.nan.\n-                if np.any(np.isnan(XBS[:, (i * n_splines) : ((i + 1) * n_splines)])):\n+                # outputs np.nan.\n+                if (use_sparse and np.any(np.isnan(XBS_sparse.data))) or (\n+                    not use_sparse\n+                    and np.any(\n+                        np.isnan(XBS[:, (i * n_splines) : ((i + 1) * n_splines)])\n+                    )\n+                ):\n                     raise ValueError(\n                         \"X contains values beyond the limits of the knots.\"\n                     )\n@@ -995,21 +1057,29 @@ def transform(self, X):\n                 # Only the first degree and last degree number of splines\n                 # have non-zero values at the boundaries.\n \n-                # spline values at boundaries\n-                f_min = spl(xmin)\n-                f_max = spl(xmax)\n                 mask = X[:, i] < xmin\n                 if np.any(mask):\n-                    XBS[mask, (i * n_splines) : (i * n_splines + degree)] = f_min[\n-                        :degree\n-                    ]\n+                    if use_sparse:\n+                        # Note: See comment about SparseEfficiencyWarning above.\n+                        XBS_sparse = XBS_sparse.tolil()\n+                        XBS_sparse[mask, :degree] = f_min[:degree]\n+\n+                    else:\n+                        XBS[mask, (i * n_splines) : (i * n_splines + degree)] = f_min[\n+                            :degree\n+                        ]\n \n                 mask = X[:, i] > xmax\n                 if np.any(mask):\n-                    XBS[\n-                        mask,\n-                        ((i + 1) * n_splines - degree) : ((i + 1) * n_splines),\n-                    ] = f_max[-degree:]\n+                    if use_sparse:\n+                        # Note: See comment about SparseEfficiencyWarning above.\n+                        XBS_sparse = XBS_sparse.tolil()\n+                        XBS_sparse[mask, -degree:] = f_max[-degree:]\n+                    else:\n+                        XBS[\n+                            mask,\n+                            ((i + 1) * n_splines - degree) : ((i + 1) * n_splines),\n+                        ] = f_max[-degree:]\n \n             elif self.extrapolation == \"linear\":\n                 # Continue the degree first and degree last spline bases\n@@ -1018,8 +1088,6 @@ def transform(self, X):\n                 # Note that all others have derivative = value = 0 at the\n                 # boundaries.\n \n-                # spline values at boundaries\n-                f_min, f_max = spl(xmin), spl(xmax)\n                 # spline derivatives = slopes at boundaries\n                 fp_min, fp_max = spl(xmin, nu=1), spl(xmax, nu=1)\n                 # Compute the linear continuation.\n@@ -1030,16 +1098,57 @@ def transform(self, X):\n                 for j in range(degree):\n                     mask = X[:, i] < xmin\n                     if np.any(mask):\n-                        XBS[mask, i * n_splines + j] = (\n-                            f_min[j] + (X[mask, i] - xmin) * fp_min[j]\n-                        )\n+                        linear_extr = f_min[j] + (X[mask, i] - xmin) * fp_min[j]\n+                        if use_sparse:\n+                            # Note: See comment about SparseEfficiencyWarning above.\n+                            XBS_sparse = XBS_sparse.tolil()\n+                            XBS_sparse[mask, j] = linear_extr\n+                        else:\n+                            XBS[mask, i * n_splines + j] = linear_extr\n \n                     mask = X[:, i] > xmax\n                     if np.any(mask):\n                         k = n_splines - 1 - j\n-                        XBS[mask, i * n_splines + k] = (\n-                            f_max[k] + (X[mask, i] - xmax) * fp_max[k]\n-                        )\n+                        linear_extr = f_max[k] + (X[mask, i] - xmax) * fp_max[k]\n+                        if use_sparse:\n+                            # Note: See comment about SparseEfficiencyWarning above.\n+                            XBS_sparse = XBS_sparse.tolil()\n+                            XBS_sparse[mask, k : k + 1] = linear_extr[:, None]\n+                        else:\n+                            XBS[mask, i * n_splines + k] = linear_extr\n+\n+            if use_sparse:\n+                if not sparse.isspmatrix_csr(XBS_sparse):\n+                    XBS_sparse = XBS_sparse.tocsr()\n+                output_list.append(XBS_sparse)\n+\n+        if use_sparse:\n+            # TODO: Remove this conditional error when the minimum supported version of\n+            # SciPy is 1.9.2\n+            # `scipy.sparse.hstack` breaks in scipy<1.9.2\n+            # when `n_features_out_ > max_int32`\n+            max_int32 = np.iinfo(np.int32).max\n+            all_int32 = True\n+            for mat in output_list:\n+                all_int32 &= mat.indices.dtype == np.int32\n+            if (\n+                sp_version < parse_version(\"1.9.2\")\n+                and self.n_features_out_ > max_int32\n+                and all_int32\n+            ):\n+                raise ValueError(\n+                    \"In scipy versions `<1.9.2`, the function `scipy.sparse.hstack`\"\n+                    \" produces negative columns when:\\n1. The output shape contains\"\n+                    \" `n_cols` too large to be represented by a 32bit signed\"\n+                    \" integer.\\n. All sub-matrices to be stacked have indices of\"\n+                    \" dtype `np.int32`.\\nTo avoid this error, either use a version\"\n+                    \" of scipy `>=1.9.2` or alter the `SplineTransformer`\"\n+                    \" transformer to produce fewer than 2^31 output features\"\n+                )\n+            XBS = sparse.hstack(output_list)\n+        elif self.sparse_output:\n+            # TODO: Remove ones scipy 1.10 is the minimum version. See comments above.\n+            XBS = sparse.csr_matrix(XBS)\n \n         if self.include_bias:\n             return XBS\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 196270, "completion_tokens": 340}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nget_trace_artifacts(artifact_type: str = 'all', path_contains: str | None = None, name_contains: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 8):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n    - executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n    - tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n    - then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n    Args:\n    - `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n    - `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n    - `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n    - `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n    - `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n    - `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n    - `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n    - `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n    - Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nget_trace_data_dependencies(symbol_contains: str | None = None, scope_contains: str | None = None, path_contains: str | None = None, edge_type: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 12, path_limit: int = 4):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    - '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.\n    - It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n    Args:\n    - `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`.\n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope.\n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time.\n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n    - `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols.\n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n    Important behavior:\n    - If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n    - If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n    - `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n    - `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n    - `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n    - `symbol`: The symbol name.\n    - `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n    - `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n    Each edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n    - During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: get_trace_artifacts ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) artifact_type (string, optional): Optional exact filter for artifact definition type.\nAllowed values: [`all`, `function`, `method`, `class`]\n  (2) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.\n  (3) name_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.\n  (4) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset.\n  (5) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #5: get_trace_data_dependencies ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n- '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.  \n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\nArgs:\n- `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names. \n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes. \n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`. \n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope. \n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. \n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n- `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols. \n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n\nImportant behavior:\n- If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n- If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n- `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n- `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n- `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n- `symbol`: The symbol name.\n- `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n- `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n\nEach edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) symbol_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n  (2) scope_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n  (3) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n  (4) edge_type (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`.\n  (5) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges.\n  (6) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice.\n  (7) path_limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include.\n---- END FUNCTION #5 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_p