form(self, X, y=None):\n1074 |         \"\"\"Learn and apply the dimensionality reduction.\n1075 | \n1076 |         Parameters\n1077 |         ----------\n1078 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1079 |             Training samples.\n1080 | \n1081 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_targets), \\\n1082 |                 default=None\n1083 |             Targets.\n1084 | \n1085 |         Returns\n1086 |         -------\n1087 |         out : array-like or tuple of array-like\n1088 |             The transformed data `X_transformed` if `Y is not None`,\n1089 |             `(X_transformed, Y_transformed)` otherwise.\n1090 |         \"\"\"\n1091 |         return self.fit(X, y).transform(X, y)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"sparse design_matrix\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ==============================\n 3 | Lasso on dense and sparse data\n 4 | ==============================\n 5 | \n 6 | We show that linear_model.Lasso provides the same results for dense and sparse\n 7 | data and that in the case of sparse data the speed is improved.\n 8 | \n 9 | \"\"\"\n10 | \n11 | from time import time\n12 | from scipy import sparse\n13 | from scipy import linalg\n14 | \n15 | from sklearn.datasets import make_regression\n16 | from sklearn.linear_model import Lasso\n17 | \n18 | \n19 | # %%\n20 | # Comparing the two Lasso implementations on Dense data\n21 | # -----------------------------------------------------\n22 | #\n23 | # We create a linear regression problem that is suitable for the Lasso,\n24 | # that is to say, with more features than samples. We then store the data\n25 | # matrix in both dense (the usual) and sparse format, and train a Lasso on\n26 | # each. We compute the runtime of both and check that they learned the\n27 | # same model by computing the Euclidean norm of the difference between the\n28 | # coefficients they learned. Because the data is dense, we expect better\n29 | # runtime with a dense data format.\n30 | \n31 | X, y = make_regression(n_samples=200, n_features=5000, random_state=0)\n32 | # create a copy of X in sparse format\n33 | X_sp = sparse.coo_matrix(X)\n34 | \n35 | alpha = 1\n36 | sparse_lasso = Lasso(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, max_iter=1000)\n37 | dense_lasso = Lasso(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, max_iter=1000)\n38 | \n39 | t0 = time()\n40 | sparse_lasso.fit(X_sp, y)\n41 | print(f\"Sparse Lasso done in {(time() - t0):.3f}s\")\n42 | \n43 | t0 = time()\n44 | dense_lasso.fit(X, y)\n45 | print(f\"Dense Lasso done in {(time() - t0):.3f}s\")\n46 | \n47 | # compare the regression coefficients\n48 | coeff_diff = linalg.norm(sparse_lasso.coef_ - dense_lasso.coef_)\n49 | print(f\"Distance between coefficients : {coeff_diff:.2e}\")\n50 | \n51 | #\n52 | # %%\n53 | # Comparing the two Lasso implementations on Sparse data\n54 | # ------------------------------------------------------\n55 | #\n56 | # We make the previous problem sparse by replacing all small values with 0\n57 | # and run the same comparisons as above. Because the data is now sparse, we\n58 | # expect the implementation that uses the sparse data format to be faster.\n59 | \n60 | # make a copy of the previous data\n61 | Xs = X.copy()\n62 | # make Xs sparse by replacing the values lower than 2.5 with 0s\n63 | Xs[Xs < 2.5] = 0.0\n64 | # create a copy of Xs in sparse format\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_pairwise_distances_reduction/_dispatcher.py:BaseDistancesReductionDispatcher.is_usable_for.is_valid_sparse_matrix`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n107 | def is_valid_sparse_matrix(X):\n108 |             return (\n109 |                 isspmatrix_csr(X)\n110 |                 and\n111 |                 # TODO: support CSR matrices without non-zeros elements\n112 |                 X.nnz > 0\n113 |                 and\n114 |                 # TODO: support CSR matrices with int64 indices and indptr\n115 |                 # See: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/23653\n116 |                 X.indices.dtype == X.indptr.dtype == np.int32\n117 |             )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_sparse_spd_matrix`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1559 | def make_sparse_spd_matrix(\n1560 |     dim=1,\n1561 |     *,\n1562 |     alpha=0.95,\n1563 |     norm_diag=False,\n1564 |     smallest_coef=0.1,\n1565 |     largest_coef=0.9,\n1566 |     random_state=None,\n1567 | ):\n1568 |     \"\"\"Generate a sparse symmetric definite positive matrix.\n1569 | \n1570 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <sample_generators>`.\n1571 | \n1572 |     Parameters\n1573 |     ----------\n1574 |     dim : int, default=1\n1575 |         The size of the random matrix to generate.\n1576 | \n1577 |     alpha : float, default=0.95\n1578 |         The probability that a coefficient is zero (see notes). Larger values\n1579 |         enforce more sparsity. The value should be in the range 0 and 1.\n1580 | \n1581 |     norm_diag : bool, default=False\n1582 |         Whether to normalize the output matrix to make the leading diagonal\n1583 |         elements all 1.\n1584 | \n1585 |     smallest_coef : float, default=0.1\n1586 |         The value of the smallest coefficient between 0 and 1.\n1587 | \n1588 |     largest_coef : float, default=0.9\n1589 |         The value of the largest coefficient between 0 and 1.\n1590 | \n1591 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1592 |         Determines random number generation for dataset creation. Pass an int\n1593 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n1594 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1595 | \n1596 |     Returns\n1597 |     -------\n1598 |     prec : sparse matrix of shape (dim, dim)\n1599 |         The generated matrix.\n1600 | \n1601 |     See Also\n1602 |     --------\n1603 |     make_spd_matrix : Generate a random symmetric, positive-definite matrix.\n1604 | \n1605 |     Notes\n1606 |     -----\n1607 |     The sparsity is actually imposed on the cholesky factor of the matrix.\n1608 |     Thus alpha does not translate directly into the filling fraction of\n1609 |     the matrix itself.\n1610 |     \"\"\"\n1611 |     random_state = check_random_state(random_state)\n1612 | \n1613 |     chol = -np.eye(dim)\n1614 |     aux = random_state.uniform(size=(dim, dim))\n1615 |     aux[aux < alpha] = 0\n1616 |     aux[aux > alpha] = smallest_coef + (\n1617 |         largest_coef - smallest_coef\n1618 |     ) * random_state.uniform(size=np.sum(aux > alpha))\n1619 |     aux = np.tril(aux, k=-1)\n1620 | \n1621 |     # Permute the lines: we don't want to have asymmetries in the final\n1622 |     # SPD matrix\n1623 |     permutation = random_state.permutation(dim)\n1624 |     aux = aux[permutation].T[permutation]\n1625 |     chol += aux\n1626 |     prec = np.dot(chol.T, chol)\n1627 | \n1628 |     if norm_diag:\n1629 |         # Form the diagonal vector into a row matrix\n1630 |         d = np.diag(prec).reshape(1, prec.shape[0])\n1631 |         d = 1.0 / np.sqrt(d)\n1632 | \n1633 |         prec *= d\n1634 |         prec *= d.T\n1635 | \n1636 |     return prec\n```\n"}, {"content": "get_trace_artifacts\n<parameter=path_contains>sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py</parameter>\n<parameter=name_contains>SplineTransformer</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "Verify if the found locations contain all the necessary information to address the issue, and check for any relevant references in other parts of the codebase that may not have appeared in the search results. If not, continue searching for additional locations related to the issue.\nVerify that you have carefully analyzed the impact of the found locations on the repository, especially their dependencies. If you think you have solved 